# Work and trade by job (doc 30): those who keep order, rule, pray, learn and heal. Guards, soldiers, officials,
# nobles, priests, scholars and healers. Plain lines answer each other in parallel.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Guards

scene tr_guard_night_watch
when topic=work a.role=guard time=night|dusk
a : Quiet night. I hate quiet nights. | Some drunk sang under the chapel window for an hour. | Saw a light moving on the old wall.
b[guard] : Quiet's what we're paid for.
b : Better than the other kind. | Did you move him? | Did you go and look?
a : Quiet means someone's planning something. | Carried him home. He sang all the way. | Of course not. I'm not daft.

scene tr_guard_boots
when topic=work a.role=guard
a : My boots let in water. Paws soaked all shift. | The sergeant wants us shaved and brushed by dawn. | Polished this helmet for an hour. Rained on it in a minute.
b : Grease them. | Brushed? For whom? | That's the watch.
a : I did. Water likes grease. | Some captain's coming. | That's the watch, every day.

scene tr_guard_gate
when topic=work a.role=guard place=gate
a : Every cart through this gate wants to argue the toll. | A merchant tried to bribe me with a cheese. | Turned away three travellers without papers.
b : They always do. | Was it a good cheese? | Where'd they go?
a : They lose. Every time. | That's not the point. | Round the wall to the broken bit, probably.

scene tr_guard_beggar
when topic=work a.role=guard b.job=beggar
a : You can't sleep on the chapel steps. Move along. | Back again? I told you yesterday. | Here. Get some soup. Don't tell the sergeant.
b : Where am I to sleep, then? | And I came back. | Bright One keep you.
a : Not on the steps. Anywhere else. | You're stubborn, I'll give you that. | And you keep your mouth shut.

scene tr_guard_thief_chase
when topic=work a.role=guard
a : Chased a pickpocket through the market and lost him at the fish stalls. | Caught a lad with a stolen purse. Couldn't be more than ten. | Somebody's been stealing lamp oil from the street lamps.
b : Fish stink hides everything. | What did you do? | Who steals lamp oil?
a : I smell like fish now and he's gone. | Took him home to his mother. Worse than a cell. | Somebody cold. Or somebody planning.

scene tr_guard_pay
when topic=work a.role=guard b.role=guard
a : Pay's short again. | They want two of us on the bridge and there's only us. | Captain's in a temper. Avoid the yard.
b : The council takes its cut. | Then one of us stands on each end. | Again?
a : The council takes everyone's cut. | And meets in the middle for a smoke. | He's always in a temper. Today's worse.

scene tr_guard_ser_ferro
when topic=work a.role=guard region=ser_ferro
a : Another warehouse fire. Third this month. | The Couriers run their papers right past the Watch. | Captain says don't touch the merchant houses' carts.
b : Insurance, I'd bet. | Proof's another matter. | Why not?
a : Insurance and rivals. | Proof costs more than we're paid. | Because someone pays the captain more than us.

scene tr_guard_ridgemere_sump
when topic=work a.role=guard region=ridgemere
a : Nobody wants the Sump patrol. | Pulled a body out of the canal this morning. | The Houses' guards think they own the streets.
b : Draw lots. | Fell in? | They do own half of them.
a : I always lose the lots. | Nobody falls in the canal by accident. | And the other half's theirs on Restday.

scene tr_guard_fortress
when topic=work a.job=guard|soldier region=fortresses
a : Nothing on the horizon for a week. Nothing! | Stood the wall all night. Saw one owl. | The supply wagon's late again.
b : Good. Keep it that way. | Was it a dangerous owl? | We'll eat the horse.
a : Nothing makes me jumpy. | It looked at me. | Not my horse.

scene tr_guard_new_recruit
when topic=work a.role=guard b.stage=adolescent|young
a : Want to join the watch, pup? | Stand up straight, you'll be a watchwolf one day. | Don't touch the spear.
b : Can I have a spear? | Do you catch bandits? | I didn't!
a : When you're taller than it. | Sometimes. Mostly drunks. | Your paw's on it right now.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Soldiers

scene tr_soldier_drill
when topic=work a.job=soldier
a : Drilled since dawn. Left, right, left, crossbow. | Sergeant had us run the wall in full kit. | Twenty quarrels at the butts, eighteen in.
b[soldier] : You call that a drill? You should have seen the old captain.
b : Must be tired. | Show-off. | Not bad.
a : Tired's a word for it. | It wasn't my idea. | Not bad's not good.

scene tr_soldier_rumour_war
when topic=work|smalltalk a.job=soldier
a : They say the army's moving south by summer. | Officers have been in the map room all week. | New orders came by courier. Sealed.
b : They always say that. | Something's coming. | What do they say?
a : One year it'll be true. | Or someone's lost a cart. | Sealed means nobody knows. Not even them.

scene tr_soldier_barracks
when topic=work a.job=soldier place=barracks
a : The bunk under mine snores like a millrace. | Mess was grey again. Grey porridge, grey meat. | Someone pinched my good blanket.
b : Pinch his nose. | Grey's a colour. | Check the corporal's bunk.
a : He bites. | The only colour here. | Not a chance. I like living.

scene tr_soldier_kit
when topic=work a.job=soldier b.job=smith|crafter
a : My harness strap's split. | Can you fix the crossbow? The latch slips. | The quartermaster won't give me new boots.
b : Leave it, I'll stitch it. | Bring it in. | Buy them.
a : Today? I'm on watch tonight. | It slipped in drill. Nearly put one in the sergeant. | On a soldier's pay?

scene tr_soldier_home
when topic=work|family a.job=soldier band=family|spouses|friends
a : I've leave in a fortnight. Three days home. | Got a letter. Mother's not well. | Captain says no leave till the harvest.
b : Three days! | Go to her. | That's months.
a : Three days, and two of them on the road. | They won't let me. | Months of wall and porridge.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Officials

scene tr_off_ledgers
when topic=work a.job=official
a : Six ledgers to copy before the council sits. | Somebody spilled ink across the tax rolls. | Half the town hasn't paid its hearth tax.
b : Six? | Can you still read them? | Can you blame them?
a : Six, and the quill's split. | Enough to know who owes. | Blame, no. Collect, yes.

scene tr_off_council
when topic=work a.job=official|noble
a : The council argued three hours over the price of lamp oil. | They voted to mend the bridge. Again. Next year. | A petition from the tanners. About the smell.
b : And? | Again. | The tanners are complaining about the smell?
a : Postponed. | Next year never comes. | No. Everyone else is. They want it noted.

scene tr_off_bribe
when topic=work a.job=official
a : A merchant tried to slip me a mark to stamp his papers. | Someone's paying the weighmaster. I'd swear it. | The records office lost a deed. Conveniently.
b : Did you take it? | Proof? | Whose deed?
a : I took his name. | Not yet. | The one everyone's suing over.

scene tr_off_upper_accord
when topic=work a.job=official|scholar region=upper_accord
a : The Concord clerks want every petition copied three times. | Wardens brought another one in last night. Gifted, they say. | The archive's flooded in the lower stacks.
b : Three times? | Quickened? | Not the old manuscripts!
a : Three. One to file, one to lose, one to argue over. | Nobody says that word out loud here. | Some of them. They'll blame the clerks.

scene tr_off_ser_ferro_court
when topic=work a.job=official|noble region=ser_ferro
a : The Royal Hand's office wants every grain ledger by Restday. | A Concord Representative's visiting court. Everyone's polishing. | Another records clerk resigned. That's four.
b : Why the grain? | Polishing what? | Four?
a : Someone's selling the Crown's stores. | Everything, including their stories. | Four. Nobody says why.

scene tr_off_toll
when topic=work a.job=official|guard place=gate|work region=towns
a : Toll takings are down a third this season. | A carter swore blind he'd paid at the last post. | I've a box of foreign coin no one will change.
b : Fewer carts. | Had he? | Melt it.
a : Or more smugglers. | He had the receipt. Forged. | I'd hang for it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Nobles

scene tr_noble_estate
when topic=work a.job=noble
a : The steward's accounts don't add up. | Our tenants want the rents lowered again. | The estate roof needs mending and the treasury's empty.
b[servant] : I'm sure there's a simple explanation, my lady. My lord.
b : Have them checked. | Can you afford it? | Sell a painting.
a : I had them checked. The checker's the steward's cousin. | I can't afford not to. | Which one? They're all of grandfather.

scene tr_noble_houses
when topic=work|smalltalk a.job=noble b.job=noble
a : House Brinewater's been courting the Fells. | Your cousin snubbed me at the feast. | The council seat goes to whoever pays most.
b : Let them. Fell timber, Brinewater hulls. It suits. | She snubs everyone. | That's how it's always gone.
a : It suits them. It doesn't suit us. | Not with her teeth showing. | Then we'll pay most.

scene tr_noble_hunting
when topic=smalltalk|work a.job=noble
a : The hunt runs at dawn. You'll run with us? | Lost my best hawk over the river. | The deer park's been poached.
b : Gladly. | Can you get her back? | By whom?
a : Wear something that doesn't clash. | She'll come back. She always does, eventually. | By whoever's hungry. Which is everyone.

scene tr_noble_petition
when topic=work a.job=noble b.role=civilian
a : State your business. Briefly. | Another petition about the mill? | You're the one who wrote to my steward.
b : The well on our street's run dry, my lord. | The miller cheats us, my lady. | I am. About the rent.
a : I'll send someone. Eventually. | Every miller cheats. Prove it. | My steward handles rents. Go away.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Priests

scene tr_priest_sermon
when topic=work a.job=priest
a : Half the benches empty at morning prayers. | Wrote a sermon on duty. They'll sleep through it. | The chapel roof's dripping on the altar.
b[old] : When I was young we filled every bench and the doorway.
b : It's cold. They stay abed. | Duty's a sleepy word. | Bright One's tears.
a : The Bright One rose earlier. | Then I'll shout it. | Rain's tears. Mend it with tiles.

scene tr_priest_confession
when topic=work a.job=priest
a : Someone came to the chapel at midnight to unburden themselves. | Heard more sins today than I've had suppers. | A child asked me where the Dark One sleeps.
b : Who? | Anything good? | What did you say?
a : That's between them and the Bright One. | Nothing a priest repeats. | Nowhere near here. I hope.

scene tr_priest_alms
when topic=work a.job=priest b.job=beggar|child
a : There's bread at the chapel door after prayers. | Come in out of the rain. | Have you eaten today?
b : For anyone? | They won't throw me out? | Not since yesterday.
a : For anyone who stays for the prayer. | Not while I'm here. | Then sit. Prayer can wait.

scene tr_priest_funeral
when topic=work a.job=priest
a : Third burial this week. | Buried a fisher with no body. Just his name. | The family can't pay for the rites.
b : A bad week. | That's hard. | Do it anyway.
a : A bad season, it's turning into. | The sea keeps the rest. | I always do. Don't tell the bishop.

scene tr_priest_concord
when topic=work a.job=priest region=accord
a : Concord inspectors examined the chapel books. | Pilgrims slept on the cathedral floor again. | The Wardens asked to see our birth records.
b : Find anything? | Every year more. | Why?
a : A few spelling mistakes. They noted them. | Faith is crowded. | They didn't say. They never do.

scene tr_priest_tide_chapel
when topic=work a.job=priest region=rain_coast|lake
a : Cut another name into the chapel wall today. | Blessed the boats at dawn. Half the fleet didn't come. | The fishers want a prayer against the fog.
b : Who? | Too early for them. | There's a prayer for that?
a : Young one. Twenty. | Fish don't care about prayers either. | There is now.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Scholars

scene tr_scholar_manuscript
when topic=work a.job=scholar
a : This manuscript's four hundred years old and someone's used it as a table mat. | I've been reading by one candle for a week. | Found a page of the old chronicle no one's seen in a century.
b : Can it be saved? | Your eyes! | What does it say?
a : Most of it. Not the soup stain. | My eyes are for reading. | Nothing good about the Bright One's critics.

scene tr_scholar_ink
when topic=work a.job=scholar|official
a : My quill keeps splitting in my teeth. | Ink froze in the pot overnight. | Copied a whole page and then sneezed on it.
b : Bite softer. | Warm it by the fire. | Bless you.
a : I'll bite the next one through. | It's between my paws now. | Bless the page. It needs it.

scene tr_scholar_argument
when topic=work|smalltalk a.job=scholar b.job=scholar|priest
a : The old cities were built by the Bright One's own paws. It's written. | The chronicle has two dates for the founding. | I think the Dark One was a person once.
b : Written by whom? | One's wrong. | Careful who hears that.
a : By the faithful, obviously. | Or both are. | I said once. Not now.

scene tr_scholar_pupil
when topic=work a.job=scholar b.stage=young|adolescent
a : Recite the seven virtues. | Hold the quill in your teeth, not your lip. | What year was the Concord founded?
b : Order, duty, um... | It hurts. | Before I was born?
a : Order, duty, community, and four more you'll learn by supper. | It hurts less with practice. | Considerably before. Read the page.

scene tr_scholar_ser_ferro
when topic=work a.job=scholar region=ser_ferro
a : The royal library won't lend to commoners now. | The court historian rewrote last year already. | Bought a book of maps at the wharf. Half the rivers are wrong.
b : Since when? | Already? | Only half?
a : Since a commoner lent a book to the wrong wolf. | Kings like their history fresh. | Half's better than most.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Healers

scene tr_heal_fever
when topic=work a.job=healer
a : A fever's going through the lower street. | Set a broken leg this morning. Clean break, he'll walk. | Up all night with a whelping.
b : Bad one? | Lucky lad. | How many pups?
a : Bad enough. Boil your water. | Lucky he fell off a low roof. | Five. All squalling.

scene tr_heal_herbs_short
when topic=work|prices a.job=healer
a : I'm out of willow bark. Can't treat a headache. | Somebody bought every bit of feverfew in the market. | The good moss only grows in the fens.
b[farmer] : There's willow down by my ditch. Take what you need.
b : Try the herb woman. | Who? | Go to the fens, then.
a : The herb woman's who I buy from. | Someone with a fever, I'd hope. | And leave the sick for a week?

scene tr_heal_gifted
when topic=work|smalltalk a.job=healer
a : There's a gifted healer in the city, they say. Closes a cut with a touch. | Folk think I should be able to heal with a word. | I'll stick to stitches and poultices.
b : Is it true? | Can't you? | Stitches work.
a : True, and it costs her. Everything costs. | I've a needle and a strong stomach. | Every time.

scene tr_heal_miner
when topic=work a.job=healer b.job=miner|laborer|smith
a : Let me see that paw. | You're coughing worse. | Keep that bandage dry this time.
b : It's nothing. | It's just the dust. | I'll try.
a : Nothing's swollen up like a plum. | Dust doesn't make you spit blood. | Trying is how it gets infected.

scene tr_heal_bonesetter
when topic=work a.job=healer region=marches
a : Two from the pits today. Crushed paws, both of them. | The long bench is full. I'm out of splints. | Set a shoulder with my weight on his chest. He swore at me in three tongues.
b : The pits eat wolves. | Use fence rails. | Did it work?
a : And spit out the bones for me. | I have been. | It worked. He's still swearing.

scene tr_heal_fens
when topic=work a.job=healer region=fens
a : Fenhollow moss stops bleeding better than any salve. | Collected bog myrtle all morning. Legs black to the shoulder. | The marsh fever's back with the warm weather.
b : Can you sell it in the city? | Smells nice, at least. | Already?
a : They pay a penny for what's worth a mark. | Nice for a day. Then it's just bog. | Every summer. Like a cousin.
